WEBVTT

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:06.490
In a previous video, we walked through
the steps for creating a single dot.

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:11.580
Let's go one step further
and explore adding rows of dots.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:16.500
First, let's think of this stitch
pattern as a series of units.

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.040
In this example,

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:23.900
each unit consists of a single row
plus a dot in contrasting color.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:29.340
The contrasting color is represented by
both light and dark blue in this diagram.

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:31.940
Gray are stitches that are not knit.

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:35.940
Remember, we're working short
rows and partial knitting.

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:40.220
And white in this diagram
is the main color.

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:45.300
This entire gray section represents
a single row of knitting.

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:47.140
The dots are seven stitches,

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:53.840
an odd number, and there are eleven
stitches, an odd number between the dots.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:57.740
The rows of dots are
separated by the main color.

00:00:57.770 --> 00:01:03.440
In this example, it's 10 rows
shown in white on the diagram.

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:10.490
To make this stitch pattern interesting,
the dots are alternated on the next row.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:13.640
I've started by casting on and knitting 10

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:17.740
rows in the main color,
ending with the carriage on the right.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:20.490
Remove the main yarn and put it aside.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:23.180
We'll pick it up again.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:28.760
The next steps will create
a single row of knitting.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.720
Keep that in mind.
I

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:35.200
plan to position the first dot five
stitches from the edge,

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:43.560
and then repeat dot units across the row
with 11 stitches between each dot.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.490
So I'll count five plus seven needles,

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:51.490
and then put the rest of the needles
on the needle bed in hold.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.420
Set the carriage to hold.

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:56.620
Thread the carriage
with the contrasting yarn.

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:59.980
Knit across these 12 stitches and wrap.

00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:06.340
Now I want to concentrate
on forming the dot.

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:09.000
I'll put those first five needles in hold

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:15.120
position and put two more needles
in hold opposite the carriage.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.450
There are five needles in work.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:20.020
Knit to the right and wrap.

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:24.840
Notice I'm adding a claw
weight in that dot section.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:30.420
Opposite the carriage, put two needles in
hold and there are three needles in work.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:33.400
Knit across and wrap.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:36.500
Opposite the carriage,
put one needle in hold.

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:41.100
There are two needles in work,
knit across and wrap.

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:44.000
That completes the dot.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:48.820
Let's look at working the next unit.

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:53.900
We'll put the two held dot needles
on the left into work.

00:02:53.930 --> 00:03:00.700
There should be four needles in work,
plus put 11 needles for your spacer,

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:04.940
plus seven needles for the next
dot into working position.

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:08.180
So you'll have a total of 22 needles.

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:13.240
The needles to the left are
future dots and remain in hold.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:19.680
Still working with the contrasting yarn,
knit across these 22 needles and wrap.

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:23.280
The carriage will be on the left.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:27.900
Now, count seven needles in work
next to the working yarn.

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:30.400
This is our new dot.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:37.920
We'll isolate that second dot by putting
all needles but those seven into hold.

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:40.540
Start forming the second dot by putting

00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:50.240
two needles opposite the carriage
into hold. Knit across and wrap.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:57.700
Opposite the carriage,
put two needles in hold.

00:03:57.730 --> 00:03:59.860
Now there are three needles in work.

00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:02.680
Knit across and wrap.

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:14.900
Opposite the carriage,
put one needle in hold.

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:17.420
And there are two needles left in work.

00:04:17.450 --> 00:04:19.620
Knit across and wrap.

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:22.580
That finishes the second dot.

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:25.380
Your carriage should be on the right.

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:27.880
Prepare for the third dot by putting

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:31.140
needles into work as before
following your diagram.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:34.980
Again, there should be
a total of 22 needles in work.

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:40.520
The needles to the left are future dots,
and they remain in hold.

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:46.380
Still working with the contrasting yarn,
knit across these 22 needles and wrap.

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:49.980
When the last dot of this first row is
complete,

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:53.920
put all needles in hold position,
cut the contrasting yarn,

00:04:53.950 --> 00:05:04.080
and free pass to pick up the main
color yarn, and then knit 10 rows.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:13.620
We're ready to start the next dot row.

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:18.500
Remove the main yarn from the feeder
and set the carriage to hold.

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:23.700
We want the new dots to be
positioned between the previous dots.

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:30.540
According to my chart, I'll have 14
stitches plus 7 for the first unit.

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:35.260
Put all needles except
these 21 stitches in hold.

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:45.160
Thread up the contrasting yarn
and knit across these 21 stitches.

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:47.700
Now, identify the seven dot needles

00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:52.180
and put the others in hold,
and then start forming your dot.

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:55.540
Opposite the carriage,
put two more needles in hold.

00:05:55.570 --> 00:05:57.940
There'll be five needles in work.

00:05:57.970 --> 00:06:02.160
Knit right and wrap.

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:07.820
Opposite the carriage,
put two more needles in hold.

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:09.980
There are three needles in work.

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:13.300
Knit left and wrap.

00:06:13.330 --> 00:06:16.220
Opposite the carriage,
put one needle in hold.

00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:18.300
There are now two needles in work.

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:21.920
Knit right and wrap.

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:29.960
As before, put two dot needles back
into work, plus needles for the next unit.

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:34.200
Which is 11 plus 7.

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:45.200
Knit across and wrap.

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:56.860
Short row as before,
forming the second dot.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:06.580
Continue working the units across
the row to complete this second dot row.

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:10.420
When the last dot is complete,
cut the contrasting yarn,

00:07:10.450 --> 00:07:15.640
free pass to pick up the main
color, and knit 10 rows.

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:20.240
Alternating the dots adds
interest to your knitting.

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:24.200
The best way to master the stitch pattern

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:32.260
is to learn to do one unit and then
think in terms of units across the row.

00:07:32.290 --> 00:07:36.020
It's best to work out your
stitch pattern on graph paper.

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:39.420
Be sure to use odd numbers for the dots

00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:45.020
and odd numbers of needles
for the stitches in between.

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:46.820
One more thing to consider,

00:07:46.850 --> 00:07:51.740
you don't want to create bobbles
by adding too many rows in the dots.

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:56.940
This photo was taken before blocking,
and you can see the raised dots.

00:07:56.970 --> 00:07:59.220
So take care as to how many rows

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:03.960
of the dots you're knitting
for a single row of this stitch pattern.

